## Proof

**Claim.** A continuous image of a Polish space can carry the indiscrete topology, and hence need not be Hausdorff.

**Key definitions.** A *Polish space* is a separable, completely metrizable topological space. A topology on a set $Y$ is *indiscrete* (trivial) if the only open sets are $\varnothing$ and $Y$.

**Step 1. The indiscrete topology on a set with $\geq 2$ points is not Hausdorff.**

If $|Y| \geq 2$ and $Y$ carries the indiscrete topology, then the only nonempty open set is $Y$ itself. Given any two distinct points $a, b \in Y$, every open neighborhood of $a$ is $Y$ and every open neighborhood of $b$ is $Y$, so no pair of disjoint open sets can separate $a$ and $b$. Thus $Y$ is not Hausdorff.

**Step 2. Construction of an indiscrete continuous image of a Polish space.**

Let $X = \mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology. This is a Polish space (it is separable and completely metrizable via the standard metric $d(x,y)=|x-y|$).

Let $Y = \{0, 1\}$ equipped with the indiscrete topology $\{\varnothing, Y\}$.

Define $f \colon \mathbb{R} \to Y$ by
$$f(x) = \begin{cases} 0 & \text{if } x < 0, \\ 1 & \text{if } x \geq 0. \end{cases}$$

**$f$ is surjective:** $f(-1) = 0$ and $f(0) = 1$, so both elements of $Y$ are attained.

**$f$ is continuous:** The only open subsets of $Y$ are $\varnothing$ and $Y$. Their preimages are
$$f^{-1}(\varnothing) = \varnothing, \qquad f^{-1}(Y) = \mathbb{R},$$
both of which are open in $\mathbb{R}$. Since the preimage of every open set is open, $f$ is continuous.

**Step 3. Conclusion.**

We have exhibited a Polish space $X = \mathbb{R}$ and a continuous surjection $f \colon X \to Y$ onto a two-point space $Y$ with the indiscrete topology. By Step 1, $Y$ is not Hausdorff. Therefore a continuous image of a Polish space can have an indiscrete topology, violating the Hausdorff property.

**Remark.** The essential reason is that continuity only constrains the preimages of open sets. When the target topology is indiscrete, the only nontrivial preimage is that of the whole space, which is always open. Hence *every* function into an indiscrete space is continuous, and any surjection from a Polish space onto a set of equal or smaller cardinality yields an indiscrete continuous image. In particular, the class of continuous images of Polish spaces is far larger than the class of Polish (or even Hausdorff) spaces; it includes all spaces of cardinality $\leq |\mathbb{R}|$ carrying the indiscrete topology.

